; AlifeTactics Reaction tuning. Paired with gamedata/scripts/at_reaction.script.
; All keys have script-side fallback defaults, so a missing key or file won't crash.
; The MCM Reaction sliders read their defaults from this file; a slider the player moved
; overrides that key, and MCM "reset to defaults" returns to these values.
;
; DIFFICULTY PARADIGM (2026-08-05): novice = the flat vanilla value on every curve (nothing sits
; below the base game, so low ranks never compound into a pushover), legend = a DECENT bump chosen
; per curve and kept well short of the game's own hardcore-aim preset (harder, not aimbot), linear
; by rank between. Vanilla rank scaling is non-functional - ai_stalker.cpp:761 clamps Rank() to
; [0,100] but Anomaly/GAMMA ranks are in the thousands, so m_fRankDisperison collapses to a constant
; and immunities/visibility ship 1.0/1.0 (game_relations.ltx) - so every curve here is a script-side
; rank axis over a FLAT baseline, not a retune of a working one.
;
;   value(tier) = novice + t(tier) * (legend - novice),  t(tier) = tier_floor / 27000
;
; src (tier positions): anomaly game_relations.ltx:8 rating = novice 0, trainee 3500,
;   experienced 6500, professional 9000, veteran 12000, expert 14500, master 17500, legend 27000.
; src (vanilla aim endpoints): xray-monolith sight_manager.cpp:75-76 g_ai_aim_min_speed 0.24,
;   g_ai_aim_min_angle PI/16 = 0.196; the game's hardcore-aim option (ui_options.script) sets
;   min_speed 2.5 / min_angle 17 - our legends stay far below that.
;
; aim_* is rad/s, the sight fine-tracking SPEED cap (select_speed, sight_manager.cpp:80-101): how
; fast the barrel moves inside the min_angle lock band. Novice 0.24 = vanilla, legend 1.50 (decent,
; not the 2.5 hardcore). Paired with track_* below - speed alone (at the vanilla 0.196 lock band)
; barely touches hit% (forced A/B, 10x spread: no first-shot or strafing-hit% change); it matters
; once track_ widens the lock band.

[at_reaction]
aim_novice       = 0.24
aim_trainee      = 0.40
aim_experienced  = 0.54
aim_professional = 0.66
aim_veteran      = 0.80
aim_expert       = 0.92
aim_master       = 1.06
aim_legend       = 1.50

; track_* is rad/s the min_angle band (ai_aim_min_angle, sight_manager.cpp:76): the gap size below
; which the barrel tracks at min_speed with no deceleration. Vanilla 0.196 is half the fire cone, so
; the barrel eases off at the cone edge and a strafer can juke it; raising it toward the fire cone
; (0.3927) and past keeps the barrel locked across the whole firing window - the real "hard to dodge"
; dial (Tracking Lock). Novice = exactly vanilla 0.196, legend 0.40 (a bit past the fire cone, decent);
; kept under max_angle 0.785 so the natural fast swing-in survives (select_speed VERIFY(max>min)).
track_novice       = 0.196
track_trainee      = 0.222
track_experienced  = 0.245
track_professional = 0.264
track_veteran      = 0.287
track_expert       = 0.306
track_master       = 0.328
track_legend       = 0.400

; lead_* is the per-rank lead ERROR (t156): the rank's multiplier on the physically-correct lead. The
; engine leads a moving target by aim_point += target_velocity * predict_time (sight_action.cpp:383-384);
; the correct predict_time is the bullet's flight time = range / bullet_speed (the weapon section's
; bullet_speed times the loaded round's k_bullet_speed, ShootingObject.cpp:168 and Level_Bullet_Manager.cpp:66,
; so subsonic/AP ammo scales the lead). at_reaction recomputes predict_time per firing NPC on the fire seam
; (throttled 1s) as clamp(range / bullet_speed, 0, 0.5) * lead_<tier>. So the lead ADAPTS to range and
; round speed on its own; lead_* is the only rank lever. Legend 1.00 = leads true and hits movers; novice
; 2.00 over-leads (overshoots), linear between. Skill is judging the lead right, and the error compounds
; with the wide low-rank dispersion cone. Bounded and range-scaled, unlike the retired fixed 0.40->0.28
; table that over-led every rank at close range.
lead_novice       = 2.00
lead_trainee      = 1.85
lead_experienced  = 1.70
lead_professional = 1.55
lead_veteran      = 1.40
lead_expert       = 1.25
lead_master       = 1.10
lead_legend       = 1.00

; vision_* is the per-rank REACTION/detection-speed factor: it multiplies get_visible_value
; accumulation (visual_memory_manager.cpp:365-379) AFTER whichever detection stack the install runs
; (engine formula or a Lua get_visible_value functor), so it is a RELATIVE rank spread over the
; installed baseline. It is reaction - how fast a stalker turns a glimpse into a confirmed threat -
; NOT eyesight: detection TIMING only, never aim or accuracy. Every install is rank-blind at
; detection (vanilla: one [stalker_vision_*] set for all stalkers, m_stalker.ltx:76-99); this adds
; the rank axis over that baseline: novice 1.00 = vanilla (retuned 2026-08-05 so NO rank detects
; slower than the base game) to legend 1.21 (+21%), the same rank spread as the prior tuning shifted
; up to sit at-or-above vanilla instead of dipping below it.
vision_novice       = 1.00
vision_trainee      = 1.03
vision_experienced  = 1.06
vision_professional = 1.09
vision_veteran      = 1.12
vision_expert       = 1.15
vision_master       = 1.18
vision_legend       = 1.21

; vrange_* is the per-rank detection-DISTANCE factor, the twin of vision_* on the other vision axis: it
; multiplies object_visible_distance (visual_memory_manager.cpp:303-318), so it sets how far out a stalker
; BEGINS to notice a target, where vision_ sets how FAST once in sight. at_reaction registers it as the
; rank slice of at_effects_resolver's VISION_RANGE effect, combined highest-wins with a carried optic's
; factor; the resolver writes it at net_spawn via set_view_distance_factor. A tight band centred on
; vanilla: novice 1.00 = vanilla to legend 1.15, the same shape as vision speed. DORMANT until the n038 view-distance bind exists on
; the exe - the setter no-ops below it, so the curve is inert but live the moment the bind arrives.
vrange_novice       = 1.00
vrange_trainee      = 1.02
vrange_experienced  = 1.05
vrange_professional = 1.07
vrange_veteran      = 1.09
vrange_expert       = 1.11
vrange_master       = 1.13
vrange_legend       = 1.15

; qsize_* / qinterval_* are the two fire-queue scales (t154), fed to npc:set_fire_queue_scale (#603) at
; spawn: qsize_ multiplies the combat planner's burst SIZE, qinterval_ the inter-burst PAUSE
; (select_queue_params, stalker_combat_action_base.cpp:246-254). Higher rank = crisper short bursts
; (qsize < 1) at a tighter cadence (qinterval < 1). NON-DEGRADATION INVARIANT: qsize >= qinterval at every
; tier. rounds/min is proportional to size/(interval + size*dt), so qsize >= qinterval keeps rounds/min
; >= vanilla; a shorter burst drops only the dispersed TAIL rounds (past base_dispersioned_bullets_count,
; WeaponMagazined.cpp:797-800), so per-shot% rises. Volume >= vanilla + per-shot% >= vanilla => hits/min
; >= vanilla (doc/library/modding/npc-combat-effectiveness.md, the queue section). Novice 1.00/1.00 =
; exact vanilla. Small spans on purpose: this is a visible fire-behavior variation, not a lethality jump.
qsize_novice       = 1.00
qsize_trainee      = 0.97
qsize_experienced  = 0.95
qsize_professional = 0.93
qsize_veteran      = 0.91
qsize_expert       = 0.89
qsize_master       = 0.87
qsize_legend       = 0.80
qinterval_novice       = 1.00
qinterval_trainee      = 0.96
qinterval_experienced  = 0.93
qinterval_professional = 0.89
qinterval_veteran      = 0.86
qinterval_expert       = 0.82
qinterval_master       = 0.79
qinterval_legend       = 0.75
